<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=screw">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Screw</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">911–<a href="/years/609-bce">609 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A screw is a helically threaded fastener tightened or released by twisting its head, and one of the six classical simple machines. It was one of the last simple machines to be invented, first appearing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the Neo-Assyrian period (911–609 BCE) and later in Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archytas">Archytas of Tarentum</a> described it. By the 1st century BCE wooden screws drove the presses that pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and grape juice around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Metal screws became common fasteners only from the 1760s, when Job and William Wyatt patented a screw machine, and Jesse Ramsden made the first satisfactory screw-cutting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lathe">lathe</a> in 1777.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/screw.jpg" alt="Screw" width="300" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brianiac · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Screws.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Most screws are tightened by clockwise rotation, which is called a right-hand thread. Left-hand threads are used in exceptional cases, such as the left pedal of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycle</a>, where the twisting force would tend to loosen a right-hand screw. The head of a screw is usually larger than the body, which gives a bearing surface and stops the screw from being driven deeper than its length. The cylindrical part below the head is the shank, and the distance between threads is the pitch. Screws are normally made from wire in three steps: cold heading, thread rolling and a coating such as zinc to prevent corrosion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Fasteners such as dowels, pins, wedges, mortises and tenons, dovetails and nails were widespread before the screw. The screw was one of the last of the simple machines to be invented. By the 1st century BCE wooden screws were in common use around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> in presses for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and for grape juice. Metal screws used as fasteners were rare in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> before the 15th century, if known at all, and did not become common until machine tools for mass production developed toward the end of the 18th century. The first documented screwdriver appears in the Housebook of Wolfegg Castle, written between 1475 and 1490, but screwdrivers probably did not become widespread until after 1800.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Standard thread forms came late. The English engineer Sir Joseph Whitworth created the first standard in about 1841, and the British Standard Fine thread was added in 1908 because his thread was too coarse for some uses. The British Association thread was devised in 1884 and standardised in 1903, and its sizes were defined in metric terms. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the Unified Thread Standard is most common, while in much of the world the ISO metric screw thread has displaced the older systems. Whitworth threads are still used in UK scaffolding and on photographic tripods, and BA threads in railway signalling and in clocks and moving-coil meters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Screw” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
